; A balance assignment computes the posting amount from the account total.
; With an unannotated target, the amount keeps the lot annotation when the
; annotated difference is a single amount, and falls back to the stripped
; difference when it spans several lots or commodities.  With an annotated
; target, the difference is restricted to that one lot, so it is always a
; single amount and keeps its annotation, also when the target is zero or
; names a lot the account does not yet hold.
;
; The journal lives in balance-assignment-lot-amount.dat, which says why.

test -f test/regress/balance-assignment-lot-amount.dat reg Assets:Broker Assets:Stocks --lot-prices --format '%(payee): %(account) %(amount)\n'
Opening: Assets:Broker 3 AAPL
Opening: Assets:Broker 2 AAPL {$90}
Single lot left over: Assets:Broker -2 AAPL {$90}
Two lots: Assets:Stocks 10 AAPL {$100}
Two lots: Assets:Stocks 5 AAPL {$110}
Several lots: Assets:Stocks 5 AAPL
Annotated target: Assets:Stocks 2 AAPL {$100}
Other commodity: Assets:Broker 5 EUR
Annotated other lot: Assets:Stocks 3 AAPL {$120}
Annotated zero: Assets:Stocks -12 AAPL {$100}
end test
